      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "The slope of the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\). The slopes of lines that are perpendicular to each other are negatives of each other.\n\nTo find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we need to find the negative reciprocal of \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\). \n\nLet's find the negative reciprocal of \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Negative reciprocal of } -\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this by implementing the solution in Python.\n```python\n# Define the slope of the original line\nslope_original = -2 / 3\n\n# Find the slope of the line that is perpendicular\nslope_perpendicular = -1 / slope_original\n\nprint(slope_perpendicular)\n```",
