      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "The first step is to convert the equation of the line into slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$. We have:\n\\[ 2x + 3y = 6 \\]\n\\[ 3y = -2x + 6 \\]\n\\[ y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2 \\]\nThus, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nTwo lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is $-1$. Let $m_1$ be the slope of the first line and $m_2$ be the slope of the second line. We have:\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\]\nSubstitute $m_1 = -\\frac{2}{3}$:\n\\[ -\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot m_2 = -1 \\]\n\\[ m_2 = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nConcluding, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line $2x + 3y = 6$ is \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}.",
