      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, we need to find the slope of the given line $2x + 3y = 6$. To do this, we can rewrite the equation in the slope-intercept form, $y = mx + b$, where $m$ is the slope.\n\nStarting with $2x + 3y = 6$, we isolate $y$:\n\\[\n3y = -2x + 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\]\n\nFrom the equation $y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2$, we can see that the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nThe slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line. Therefore, the slope of the line perpendicular to the given line is:\n\\[\nm_{\\text{perpendicular}} = -\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to $2x + 3y = 6$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
