    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line given by the equation \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we first need to determine the slope of the given line. The equation of a line in slope-intercept form is \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope.\n\nStarting with the given equation:\n\\[2x + 3y = 6,\\]\nwe need to solve for \\(y\\). First, isolate the \\(y\\)-term on one side of the equation:\n\\[3y = -2x + 6.\\]\nNext, divide every term by 3 to solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2.\\]\nNow the equation is in slope-intercept form, \\(y = mx + b\\), where the slope \\(m\\) is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\).\n\nThe slope of a line perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line. Therefore, the slope of the line perpendicular to the line with slope \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\) is:\n\\[-\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}.\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).",
